          "stem": "Without the use of calculus, find the maximum and minimum points on the curve of \\(y=\\frac{5}{2+\\sin x+\\cos x}\\) for \\(0\\le x\\le2\\pi\\)",
          "hint": "Write sin x + cos x = √2 sin(x + π/4); a small denominator gives a large y.",
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Write \\(\\sin x+\\cos x\\) in auxiliary-angle form.",
              "work": "\\(\\sin x+\\cos x=\\sqrt2\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "So the denominator ranges between \\(2-\\sqrt2\\) and \\(2+\\sqrt2\\).",
              "work": "\\(2+\\sqrt2\\sin\\left(x+\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right):\\ \\max 2+\\sqrt2\\ \\text{at}\\ x=\\tfrac{\\pi}{4},\\quad\\min 2-\\sqrt2\\ \\text{at}\\ x=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "The fraction is largest when the denominator is smallest, and smallest when the denominator is largest.",
              "work": "\\(y_{\\max}=\\frac{5}{2-\\sqrt2}\\ \\text{at}\\ x=\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\qquad y_{\\min}=\\frac{5}{2+\\sqrt2}\\ \\text{at}\\ x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\)",
